To design a goal model for the multi-robot system described, we need to decompose the mission into a hierarchical structure of goals and tasks. The mission involves delivering food, retrieving dishes, and interacting with humans or other robots. Let's break down the mission into goals and tasks, specifying their relationships and justifications.

### Goal Model

#### Goals

| Goal ID | Goal Title                | Text                                                                 | Runtime Annotation       | Goal Type | Relation | Justification                                                                 |
|---------|---------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|-----------|----------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| G1      | Deliver Food              | Ensure food is delivered to the inpatient room.                      | -                        | Achieve   | -        | This is the main goal of the mission, ensuring delivery is successful.       |
| G2      | Handle Delivery Method    | Determine how the food will be delivered (robot tray or table).     | FALLBACK(G2.1, G2.2)     | Query     | G1       | Depending on patient capability and presence of companions, choose method.   |
| G2.1    | Deliver to Table          | Deliver food directly to the room table.                             | -                        | Perform   | G2       | Direct delivery if no need for tray retrieval.                               |
| G2.2    | Prepare for Tray Delivery | Prepare for food retrieval from the robot's tray.                    | -                        | Perform   | G2       | Necessary if tray retrieval is required.                                      |
| G3      | Track Meal Retrieval      | Track which meal is retrieved by whom and when.                      | #                        | Achieve   | G1       | Parallel tracking ensures accurate record-keeping.                            |
| G4      | Alert on Wrong Retrieval  | Alert if the wrong meal is retrieved.                                | -                        | Perform   | G1       | Immediate alerting is crucial for correction.                                 |
| G5      | Retrieve Dirty Dishes     | Retrieve dirty dishes from the room.                                 | FALLBACK(G5.1, G5.2, G5.3)| Achieve  | G1       | Multiple retrieval methods ensure dish retrieval.                             |
| G5.1    | Unassisted Dish Retrieval | Retrieve dishes without assistance.                                  | -                        | Perform   | G5       | Direct retrieval if no assistance is needed.                                  |
| G5.2    | Assistive Dish Retrieval  | Retrieve dishes with robot cooperation.                              | -                        | Perform   | G5       | Necessary when unassisted retrieval is not possible.                          |
| G5.3    | Human-Assisted Retrieval  | Retrieve dishes with human assistance.                               | -                        | Perform   | G5       | Required when robots cannot handle the task alone.                            |

#### Tasks

| Task ID | Task Title                 | Text                                                                 | Relation | Location       | Number of Robots | Justification                                                                 |
|---------|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|----------|----------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| AT1     | Navigate to Room           | Move to the designated inpatient room.                              | AND      | Hospital       | 1                | Single robot needed for navigation.                                           |
| AT2     | Deliver Food to Table      | Place food on the room table.                                        | AND      | Inpatient Room | 1                | Direct delivery requires one robot.                                           |
| AT3     | Indicate Meal Retrieval    | Indicate which meal to retrieve from the tray.                       | AND      | Inpatient Room | 1                | Single robot interaction with patient or companion.                           |
| AT4     | Track Meal Retrieval       | Log the meal retrieval details.                                      | AND      | Inpatient Room | 1                | Single robot tracks and logs retrieval.                                       |
| AT5     | Alert on Wrong Retrieval   | Sound alert if incorrect meal is retrieved.                          | AND      | Inpatient Room | 1                | Immediate alerting by one robot.                                              |
| AT6     | Retrieve Dishes Unassisted | Collect dirty dishes without assistance.                             | AND      | Inpatient Room | 1                | Single robot can handle unassisted retrieval.                                 |
| AT7     | Retrieve Dishes with Robot | Collect dirty dishes with another robot's help.                      | AND      | Inpatient Room | [2, 2]           | Two robots needed for cooperative dish retrieval.                             |
| AT8     | Retrieve Dishes with Human | Collect dirty dishes with human assistance.                          | AND      | Inpatient Room | [1, n]           | One robot works with a human, number of humans can vary.                      |

### Logical Relationships

- **G1 -> G2, G3, G4, G5 [AND]**
  - **Justification**: All sub-goals must be achieved to ensure the main goal of food delivery is successful.

- **G2 -> G2.1, G2.2 [FALLBACK]**
  - **Justification**: Depending on the patient's ability and presence of companions, either direct table delivery or tray preparation is chosen.

- **G5 -> G5.1, G5.2, G5.3 [FALLBACK]**
  - **Justification**: Multiple methods for dish retrieval ensure success regardless of conditions.

- **G1 -> AT1, AT2, AT3, AT4, AT5, G5 -> AT6, AT7, AT8 [AND]**
  - **Justification**: Each task is essential for achieving its respective goal, ensuring all necessary actions are completed.

This goal model provides a structured approach to decomposing the mission into manageable components, ensuring clarity and efficiency in multi-robot operations.
